On 2009-08-01, James Of Tucson <james0tucson@gmail.com> wrote:
> You can probably read the others safely.  Here's what you do:
>
> Wear one piece of armor that you would neither mind destroying nor
> enchanting.
> Wield a weapon that would not be useless if enchanted nor missed if
> destroyed.
> Have in your inventory a spent wand or an ability ring, something that
> can be charged.
> Don't have any scrolls or potions on you until you know ?oFire.

This is way, way too much effort.  I would suggest instead learning
to habitually price-ID the scrolls below 100zm:

enchant armor (80)
remove curse (80)
enchant weapon (60)
light (50)
identify (20)

...read-IDing to distinguish EA from RC, and saving up and blessing your 
ID scrolls to get all your items in one go.  Of the remaining scrolls, the 
ones that are actually worth bothering about are:

taming (200) (covered by 'amnesia')
genocide, charging (300) (covered by 'punishment')
teleportation, gold detection, magic mapping (100) (covered by 'fire')

Earth is also a useful scroll in the 200 range, but it is de facto
identified for you on the first level of Sokoban -- no need to worry.

Teleportation can be found in hallways and identified that way; also,
monsters will eventually read one in front of you.

Genocide, charging, and taming should never be risked via read-ID, and gold
detection and mapping are only useful later in the game, well past the
point where you've got plenty of blessed ?oID hanging around.

Scare monster is only significant for illiterates; there are too many
ways to create an engraved or burned Elbereth to make this scroll worth 
even bothering with _unless_ you're holding onto illiterate conduct.
(Pursuant to this, it is important to engrave-ID all wands immediately
on obtainment -- there are at least four that are one-turn lifesavers!)

> I'm sure there are a few more I'm forgetting, but basically, you want
> to make it safe to read an unidentified scroll, but more than this,
> you don't want that scroll to be wasted, no matter what it turns out
> to be.

Most scrolls are better off as blank paper anyway, frankly.  Then you
can use your markers to turn them into something you need at the time.

